The Philosopher's Workbench, a guide to read in Xenith

Everything here is run by clicking.  Sweep a command with button 2
(the middle button; Option and drag on a Mac trackpad) to run it; a
single word, such as Put, needs only a click.  What a command prints
goes to a window named +Errors in this directory.  Each finding there
begins with an address, such as agents.md:3; click it with button 3
(Cmd on a Mac) and Xenith opens the file at that line.  Button 3 on a
file name below opens the file.

Nothing here talks to a model except section 8, and nothing needs a
service: the workbench is commands, run as any other.

0. Once, to start

Put the workbench in this Xenith's name space:

	Local run ../philns

Local runs it in Xenith's own name space, so every command after it
finds phil/prove and the rest.  It brings in the Writer's Workbench
too, from infernode-wwb beside this tree, which reads the prose; if
your profile already runs philns, skip this.

1. A model's misreading, caught by rule

agents.md is three sentences.  A logic file says what each one means;
a model writes it (section 7), or a person.  This one is right:

	phil/prove agents.logic agents.md

argue/valid 1: the conclusion follows.  This one was written by a
model that read "only" as "if", which models do:

	phil/prove agents-misread.logic agents.md

argue/converse finds the misreading at the sentence it misreads;
argue/invalid gives the case that refutes the argument as read, and
names the fallacy (denying the antecedent); argue/missing says what
premise would make it follow.  Open the two logic files side by side
and compare P1.

2. Necessity and obligation

A classic slip, from "necessarily, if p then q" and p to
"necessarily q":

	phil/prove necessity.logic necessity.md

And from "you ought to" to "you do" (logic kd, the logic of ought):

	phil/prove ought.logic ought.md

Each says when it fails, in the sentences' own words.  Where an
argument turns on where the necessity is put, scope says so: it turns
each "if p, then necessarily q" over, p → □q against □(p → q), and
proves the argument again:

	phil/scope necessity.logic necessity.md

Read as the sentence has it, the argument fails; it needs the necessity
on "he is unmarried" itself, which the sentence does not give.  No
model is asked: the prover writes both readings.

3. An argument's shape

cosmos.md argues in steps (C1, then C), meets an objection (O1) and
answers it (R1).  The logic file says what rests on what:

	phil/map cosmos.logic

The same in Argdown, for argument-mapping tools:

	phil/map -a cosmos.logic

4. Objections and replies

	phil/prove cosmos.logic cosmos.md

The steps hold, and the conclusion survives the objection, but
argue/restates says the reply only repeats the premise the objection
disputes: it begs the question.  Take the reply away:

	phil/prove cosmos-unanswered.logic cosmos.md

The objection stands, so P1 falls, and with it C1 and C, each at its
sentence.  A reply aimed at the wrong thing:

	phil/prove cosmos-missed.logic cosmos.md

argue/misses: it does not contradict the objection.  And
argue/negation: the logic drops the "nothing" its sentence says.

5. Correcting the logic and asking again

Open agents-misread.logic (button 3 on its name).  In P1 change

	rational(x) -> moral(x)

to

	moral(x) -> rational(x)

click Put in its tag, and run the line in section 1 again: it now
holds.  This is the loop: a model or a person writes the logic, prove
decides, the file is corrected by hand, prove decides again.  (Change
P1 back after, and Put, so that the next reader sees the misreading;
or, on the host, git checkout demo.)

6. For another prover

	phil/prove -t agents.logic

The same argument in TPTP, for E, Vampire or Z3 to check.

7. Who says what

	phil/says reports.md

Each sentence that gives a claim as someone else's, and whose: by rule,
from the words' parts of speech, with no model.  "We hold" is the
writer's own, and is not listed.

8. With a model (llmsrv at /mnt/llm, a key in factotum, or $wwbmodel;
   see wwb/ask in wwb(1))

argue has the model read the document three times and write the
logic; where the readings differ, prove asks a question that tells
them apart:

	phil/argue -k 3 cosmos.md

It writes cosmos.md.logic; open it, correct it if need be, and:

	phil/prove cosmos.md.logic cosmos.md

Then whether the argument moves between two senses of one word: a
model is asked only "one sense or two?", and the prover says whether
the argument turns on it:

	phil/senses -d cosmos.md cosmos.md.logic

And whether it turns on the scope of a necessity or an ought:

	phil/scope cosmos.md.logic cosmos.md

To read a source for everything it claims, not one argument (what a
corpus holds a commentary's reports to):

	phil/claims reports.md

The model only reads; the rules decide.

The manual is man 1 phil; what the tools can and cannot do for a
philosophical problem is in ../docs/REACH.md.
